---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site-config';
import { SITES } from '../lib/sites';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd, organizationJsonLd, serviceJsonLd, faqPageJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.main}/manufacturing/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do you own the factory that makes the charcoal?',
    a: 'No. Charcoal Hub is a sourcing and product-intelligence platform; production runs at independent supplier plants. That is why this page describes gates, records and evidence labels rather than equipment and capacity. A supplier claim is published with the label its evidence supports — supplier-reported, registry-checked, sample-measured or third-party tested — and no record is promoted because a plant says so.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can you guarantee the production batch will match the sample?',
    a: 'No one can guarantee that from a sample, and a supplier who does is describing hope rather than control. A sample proves a specification can be made; it does not prove a line can hold it across a lot. The batch is protected by three things instead: a written acceptance rule that says what happens on a miss, gates during the run that catch drift early, and a retained sample from the shipped lot that makes a later comparison physical rather than verbal.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who writes the specification — you or the buyer?',
    a: 'Either, but it has to end up in one field set. A catalogue order selects a recorded size class and the specification is the supplier\'s. A built-to-specification order starts from the buyer\'s sheet. In both cases the values are restated in the same fields — dimension and tolerance, piece mass or count, moisture with its basis, ash, fixed carbon and volatiles with their methods, burn conditions, packaging and acceptance rule — so two suppliers can be compared on the same axes and a lab result can be read against the clause it belongs to.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does "supplier-reported" mean in your records?',
    a: 'It means the value came from the supplier — a datasheet, a specification sheet or a written message — and has not been independently checked. It is useful as a starting point for a sample request and useless as a contract term. Moving a value off that label needs a document that supports it: a public registry record for a legal fact, a measured sample for a piece, or a laboratory report for a tested parameter, each limited to what it actually covers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know a supplier can handle my volume before I commit?',
    a: 'Ask for a written run schedule rather than a monthly tonnage figure. A capacity number on a website is a claim about equipment; a schedule is a commitment about your order — which line, which week, what else is running, and what happens if the previous lot overruns. For a first order, keep the volume inside what one run can produce and inspect that run. Published capacity is not used on this page because no plant-level allocation has been verified.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.main}/` },
    { name: 'Manufacturing', url: canonical },
  ]),
  organizationJsonLd({
    id: SITES.main,
    name: 'Charcoal Hub',
    description: site.description,
  }),
  serviceJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    name: 'Charcoal Manufacturing Management',
    serviceType: 'Charcoal manufacturing and sourcing management',
    description: 'Charcoal manufacturing managed across qualified suppliers — specification control, stage-gate quality checks, batch records, pre-shipment inspection and an evidence label on every published value.',
    providerId: SITES.main,
  }),
  faqPageJsonLd({ id: `${canonical}#faq`, faqs }),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="Charcoal Manufacturing & Sourcing — How We Run a Batch"
  description="How charcoal manufacturing is managed across qualified suppliers: specification control, stage-gate QC, batch records and pre-shipment inspection."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'Manufacturing', url: '/manufacturing/' }]}
>
  <div class="page-title container">
    <h1>Manufacturing &amp; Sourcing</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Charcoal Hub is a sourcing and product-intelligence platform, not a single factory. Production
      runs at independent supplier plants. This page covers the part we actually control — how a run
      is specified, gated, documented and labelled — and it separates what has been checked from what
      a supplier simply states.
    </p>
    <div class="page-title-media">
      <img src="/images/page-manufacturing.webp" alt="Charcoal briquette production line with press machine and conveyor — conceptual illustration" width="1600" height="900" loading="eager" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Two Ways a Charcoal Order Gets Made</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Almost every charcoal order is one of two things, and the difference decides where the risk
          sits. A <strong>catalogue order</strong> selects a product that already exists: a recorded
          size class, a grade the supplier runs, a packaging format they stock. A
          <strong>built-to-specification order</strong> creates the product for the order — a raw
          material, shape, dimension, grade or pack that the plant does not run by default.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first is faster and cheaper to start, and the risk is mostly that the catalogue
          description and the delivered lot disagree. The second puts the engineering in your hands:
          the risk moves to whether the specification was written tightly enough to be reproducible,
          and whether the sample that convinced you came from the line that will make the goods. Most
          first orders are the first kind; most private-label programmes end up as the second.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Question</th><th>Catalogue order</th><th>Built-to-specification order</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>What is fixed</td><td class="spec-value">A product the plant already runs</td><td class="spec-value">A specification written for the order</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Who defines the values</td><td class="spec-value">The supplier's record</td><td class="spec-value">The buyer's sheet, restated in a shared field set</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What the sample proves</td><td class="spec-value">The lot resembles the catalogue item</td><td class="spec-value">The specification is manufacturable</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Where variation enters</td><td class="spec-value">Batch-to-batch drift on a known product</td><td class="spec-value">Material lot, tooling, drying and pack, all at once</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What has to be approved</td><td class="spec-value">The grade and the pack</td><td class="spec-value">The specification, the sample and the artwork</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          If your order is the first kind, the
          <a href="/private-label/">private label page</a> follows the pack and artwork side. If it is
          the second, the <a href="/oem/">OEM page</a> covers tooling ownership and the sample path in
          detail. Both end up here, because both have to be produced and checked the same way. If the
          product and volume are already decided, start with a
          <a href="/request-quote/">quote request</a> and state the destination — the shipping terms
          change the packing decision before production does.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Who Does What on a Run</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A manufacturing order fails quietly when everyone assumes someone else owns a decision. The
          split below is the one used on a Charcoal Hub order; a direct factory purchase usually
          collapses the middle column into the buyer, which is why the buyer ends up chasing tooling,
          sampling and inspection at the same time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Decision</th><th>Buyer</th><th>Sourcing desk</th><th>Factory</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Product specification</td><td class="spec-value">Sets the requirement and the target market</td><td class="spec-value">Restates it in the shared field set; flags untestable clauses</td><td class="spec-value">Confirms feasibility against the line</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Supplier selection</td><td class="spec-value">Approves the shortlist</td><td class="spec-value">Document review, sample request, status label</td><td class="spec-value">Provides documents and a sample</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sample approval</td><td class="spec-value">Signs the golden sample and its measurement record</td><td class="spec-value">Checks the record against the specification</td><td class="spec-value">Produces the sample to the stated limits</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tooling and print plates</td><td class="spec-value">Pays, and holds ownership if the clause says so</td><td class="spec-value">Writes the tooling annex</td><td class="spec-value">Cuts, holds and maintains the tool</td></tr>
            <tr><td>In-process control</td><td class="spec-value">Reviews the gate records</td><td class="spec-value">Checks that each gate has a result and a signer</td><td class="spec-value">Runs the checks and keeps the logs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pre-shipment inspection</td><td class="spec-value">Accepts or rejects against the acceptance rule</td><td class="spec-value">Commissions the inspection and holds the retained sample</td><td class="spec-value">Makes the lot available and fixes a miss</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>The Line, in Brief</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Coconut shell hookah charcoal runs through the same six stages almost everywhere. Each stage
          fixes one property, and each has a characteristic way of going wrong — which is what makes it
          worth a check rather than an assumption.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it fixes</th><th>How it fails</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Shell intake and storage</td><td class="spec-value">Feedstock moisture, contamination</td><td class="spec-value">Mould, soil and sand raising ash</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Carbonisation</td><td class="spec-value">Fixed carbon, volatiles, yield</td><td class="spec-value">Under-char smokes; over-char goes friable</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Milling and screening</td><td class="spec-value">Particle size distribution</td><td class="spec-value">Uneven density and weak pieces</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Binder and mixing</td><td class="spec-value">Green strength, ash contribution</td><td class="spec-value">Cracks in drying, extra ash in the product</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pressing and cutting</td><td class="spec-value">Density, dimension, shape</td><td class="spec-value">Size drift as the die wears; delamination</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Drying and conditioning</td><td class="spec-value">Final moisture, hardness</td><td class="spec-value">Case-hardened outside, wet inside; mould in transit</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sorting and packing</td><td class="spec-value">Count, breakage, pack integrity</td><td class="spec-value">Short count, fines in the box</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This page does not repeat the process detail. The
          <a href="/hookah-charcoal-manufacturing/">hookah charcoal manufacturing guide</a> runs each
          stage in sequence, ties it to the parameter it sets and the defect it produces, and explains
          why batch-to-batch consistency is a different problem from conformance. Read that guide if
          you are writing a specification; read this page if you are placing a run.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>QC Gates: Where a Batch Is Allowed to Continue</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A gate is not the same thing as a test. A test produces a number; a gate produces a decision,
          a record and a named person who accepted it. A plant can test every batch and still ship a
          bad lot if no test is attached to a rule that says what happens when the number is outside
          the limit. Six gates cover a coconut shell charcoal run.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Gate</th><th>When</th><th>What is checked</th><th>Record</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Feedstock intake</td><td class="spec-value">On receipt at the plant</td><td class="spec-value">Moisture, shell type, visible soil and foreign matter</td><td class="spec-value">Intake log with supplier and lot</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Carbonisation outturn</td><td class="spec-value">End of each kiln cycle</td><td class="spec-value">Yield, appearance, volatile matter by the agreed method</td><td class="spec-value">Batch card tied to the kiln cycle</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mix and binder</td><td class="spec-value">Every mix</td><td class="spec-value">Binder ratio, water, particle blend</td><td class="spec-value">Mix log with weights</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Green piece</td><td class="spec-value">Start of the press run</td><td class="spec-value">Dimension, piece mass, green strength</td><td class="spec-value">First-article measurement</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dried finished piece</td><td class="spec-value">End of drying</td><td class="spec-value">Moisture, dimension, piece mass, drop breakage</td><td class="spec-value">Drying record plus a sealed sample</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pre-shipment</td><td class="spec-value">Before loading</td><td class="spec-value">Count, dimension sample, moisture, breakage, marks</td><td class="spec-value">Inspection report and retained sample</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Two details decide whether these gates mean anything. The first is the sampling rule: how
          many pieces, drawn from where, measured with what. A tolerance without a sampling plan cannot
          be enforced, because any single piece can be found that passes. Public inspection
          conventions such as ISO 2859-1 describe how to size a sample by lot and acceptance level;
          they are a starting point for the purchase order, not a substitute for one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second is the retained sample. One sealed unit from the shipped lot, kept by both sides,
          is what turns a later complaint into a comparison. Without it, a dispute is a memory of what
          the sample looked like months earlier — and the party holding the stock usually wins that
          argument.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>One Specification Model Across Suppliers</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Multi-supplier sourcing only works if the offers are comparable. We restate every product in
          one field set — dimension with a tolerance, piece mass or count per kilogram, moisture with
          its basis, ash, fixed carbon and volatiles with their test methods, burn conditions,
          <a href="/packaging/">pack format</a> and the acceptance rule — so a buyer compares the same axes instead of two suppliers'
          differently-worded sheets.
        </p>
        <p>
          Within that model, two habits prevent most disputes. Separate a <em>limit</em> from a
          <em>typical</em>: “≤ 3.5% ash, typical 3.0%” tells the factory what fails and what to aim
          for, while one number hides which it is. And attach a method and a basis to every measured
          value, because the same sample reads differently under different methods and a moisture
          figure means nothing without knowing whether it is as-received or dry.
        </p>
        <p>
          The definitions live on the
          <a href={`${SITES.data}/specifications/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">specification pages</a>,
          the standards behind them on the
          <a href={`${SITES.testing}/tests/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">test method reference</a>,
          and the field-by-field version of this argument in the
          <a href="/coconut-shell-charcoal-guide/">coconut shell specification guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Supplier Qualification, and What It Is Worth</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A supplier is qualified in four steps, and each step supports a different statement. Document
          review checks the paperwork that underpins a claim — business licence, factory documentation,
          product specifications, certifications — for consistency rather than for impressiveness.
          Sample evaluation measures a physical piece against the intended application. Batch testing
          checks production output, and only a third-party laboratory moves a parameter to
          independently verified. On-site or third-party verification is what can support a capability
          claim about the plant itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          The current state of the directory is worth stating plainly: of the eight supplier profiles
          published, three are <strong>Supplier Reported</strong> and five are
          <strong>Unverified</strong>. None is Verified or Third-Party Verified. The one Verified record
          on the manufacturer site is the operating entity itself, and it is excluded from supplier
          counts because it is not a charcoal plant. A directory that reads better than its evidence is
          worse than a short one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The workflow, the status labels and the reason a storefront cannot earn one are set out on
          the <a href={`${SITES.manufacturer}/how-we-qualify/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">qualification model page</a>
          and the <a href={`${SITES.manufacturer}/verification/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">verification model</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>What Is Supplier-Declared — and What Each Source Can Support</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Most charcoal data in circulation comes from the seller. That is not automatically wrong, but
          it is a different kind of statement from a measurement, and the two should never be printed
          in the same typeface. Every value published on this site carries the label its source
          supports.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Source</th><th>What it can support</th><th>What it cannot</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Supplier datasheet or written statement</td><td class="spec-value">A stated value, labelled supplier-reported</td><td class="spec-value">Independent confirmation, or consistency across batches</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Public registry record</td><td class="spec-value">Legal existence, registration data, company status</td><td class="spec-value">Product capability, quality or trading volume</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sample measurement</td><td class="spec-value">The measured piece, at that moment, by that method</td><td class="spec-value">The production lot, or the next order</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Third-party laboratory report</td><td class="spec-value">The tested parameters, for the sampled lot</td><td class="spec-value">The factory, other batches or other products</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Inspection report</td><td class="spec-value">Count, condition and marks of the inspected lot</td><td class="spec-value">Chemistry unless samples were tested</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Three consequences follow. A 26 mm size class carrying a piece count is not a chemistry
          record, and should not be quoted as one. A certificate of analysis is a document about a
          sample, not a factory audit — the
          <a href="/charcoal-testing-coa-guide/">testing and COA guide</a> explains how to read what it
          actually covers. And a supplier's monthly tonnage is a claim about equipment until a run
          schedule names your order.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Capacity, Minimums and Lead Time</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          No plant capacity is published on this page, because a capacity figure without an allocation
          is a number about someone else's equipment. What matters for a purchase is narrower: which
          line, which week, what else is booked, and what the plant does if the previous lot overruns.
          Those answers belong in a written run schedule, and they are confirmed at quotation for the
          specific product and volume.
        </p>
        <p>
          Minimums follow the order type. As a public planning reference, stock-packaging runs are
          commonly described at around one tonne, while a printed private-label order typically needs a
          full 20 ft container because the print setup is charged to the run. A custom mould can add a
          minimum of its own. The effective minimum is the largest of these, not an average.
        </p>
        <p>
          For volume planning, a 20 ft container of coconut shell hookah charcoal loads at roughly 14.4
          tonnes and a 40 ft high-cube at roughly 23.4 tonnes before retail packaging and pallets take
          their share. Production is commonly quoted at 15–25 days after artwork approval for a printed
          order. All three figures are industry reference, not commitments, and your
          <a href="/request-quote/">quote request</a> is where the schedule for a specific run is
          confirmed. The
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/coconut-charcoal-moq/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">MOQ guide</a>
          and the <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-container-loading/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">container-loading guide</a>
          work through the arithmetic.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Documents That Travel With the Goods</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A run is not finished when the pallets are wrapped. The document set is part of the delivery,
          and a missing one can stop a container at destination more reliably than a quality argument.
          A commercial invoice and a packing list that matches carton and pallet marks are the
          baseline. A certificate of analysis belongs with them when a parameter was tested. Charcoal
          moves under UN 1361, Class 4.2, so the dangerous-goods declaration and safety data sheet
          travel in the same file.
        </p>
        <p>
          Destination rules may add a certificate of origin, labelling requirements or an import
          permit, and the importer of record is the party that carries them. The
          <a href="/export/">export page</a> covers the shipping side, and the
          <a href={`${SITES.knowledge}/knowledge/charcoal-import-certifications/`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">import certification guide</a>
          lists what different markets typically ask for.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-pending">
        <span class="callout-title">Data pending — what this page does not claim</span>
        <p>
          No factory, production capacity, certification, client or price is stated here, because none
          is verified in the records. The three Supplier Reported and five Unverified profiles are the
          current state of the directory, not a placeholder to be upgraded by copywriting. Container
          loads, the one-tonne stock minimum, the printed-order minimum and the 15–25 day production
          window are industry reference or planning figures. Capacity and lead time for your order are
          confirmed in writing at quotation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Manufacturing Questions</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-banner">
        <h2>Have a specification and a shipment date?</h2>
        <p>
          Send the product, dimension and tolerance, target market and first volume — we will confirm
          which stages can be quoted, which need a sample, and which values are still supplier-reported.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/request-quote/">Start a Sourcing Enquiry →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
